# AVIS CALL

A global communication platform: rooms, global + private chat, private/group voice and video calls, and a server-validated credit system.

## Why this isn't a single-file Netlify drop

Your usual apps are one HTML file with Firebase, drag-dropped to Netlify. AVIS CALL can't work that way because:
- **Calls need a signaling server that stays connected** (WebSocket/Socket.io) to relay WebRTC offers/answers/ICE candidates. Netlify Functions are request/response only — they can't hold a live connection.
- **Credits must be enforced server-side.** If the balance lived only in the browser, anyone could edit it in devtools. A backend with a service-role database key is what makes that impossible.

So this ships as three pieces instead of one file:

```
avis-call/
├── database/schema.sql     → run once in Supabase
├── backend/                → Node.js + Socket.io signaling/API server (deploy to Render/Railway)
└── frontend/
    ├── index.html           → the user-facing app (deploy to Netlify, same as always)
    └── admin.html           → the admin dashboard (deploy alongside it, e.g. /admin.html)
```

## Setup (in order)

### 1. Supabase (database + auth) — free tier is fine to start
1. Create a project at supabase.com.
2. Open the SQL Editor, paste in `database/schema.sql`, run it.
3. Project Settings → API: copy your **Project URL**, **anon public key**, and **service_role key** (keep the service_role key secret — it never goes in the frontend).
4. Auth → Providers: email/password is enabled by default, which is all this app uses.
5. To make your own account an admin: after you sign up once in the app, run in SQL Editor:
   ```sql
   update public.users set is_admin = true where id = 'your-user-uuid';
   ```

### 2. TURN server (needed for calls to work reliably)
STUN alone (free, already wired in) gets most calls connected, but calls between two people on strict mobile-carrier NAT (common in Nigeria) will fail without a TURN relay. Options, easiest first:
- **Metered.ca** — free tier (20GB/month), sign up, copy the TURN URL/username/credential into your backend `.env`.
- **Twilio STUN/TURN** — pay-as-you-go, very reliable.
- **Self-hosted coturn** — free but you manage a VPS; only worth it once you have real usage.

Without any TURN configured, the app still works but some calls just won't connect — it will not fail silently, callers will see a stuck "Connecting…" state.

### 3. Backend
```
cd backend
cp .env.example .env    # fill in Supabase keys + TURN keys
npm install
npm start                # or `npm run dev` with nodemon
```
Deploy to **Render** (easiest): New → Web Service → connect this folder → build command `npm install`, start command `npm start` → add the same env vars in Render's dashboard. Railway or Fly.io work the same way. Netlify/Vercel will NOT work for this piece (no persistent WebSocket support on their standard tiers).

### 4. Frontend
1. Open `frontend/index.html` and `frontend/admin.html`, fill in the `CONFIG` object at the top of each with your Supabase URL/anon key and your deployed backend URL.
2. Drag-and-drop the `frontend` folder into Netlify, same as your other apps.

## What's implemented vs. what's scaffolded

**Fully wired end-to-end:** auth, room create/join/password/block/remove/close, global + private real-time chat with per-message credit deduction, credit balance/packages/redemption, low-credit and zero-credit warnings, offline queueing, admin stats/rates/packages/reports, atomic server-side credit ledger (`credit_transactions` table = full audit trail).

**Working but worth hardening before real users:** WebRTC calling (private 1:1 fully wired; group calls use a mesh topology — fine for small rooms, but if you expect >6-8 people on a group video call at once you'll want an SFU like LiveKit or mediasoup instead of raw mesh WebRTC, since mesh means every participant uploads N-1 video streams simultaneously).

**Not built (flagged, not faked):** push notifications for incoming calls when the app is backgrounded/closed (needs a service worker + web push, or wrapping in something like Capacitor for real mobile push), end-to-end encryption of chat/call content (current security is transport-level HTTPS/WSS + auth, not E2EE), and payment processing for credit packages (the redeem endpoint currently just grants credits — wire it to Paystack/Flutterwave before charging real money for packages).

## Credit system, honestly

Per your spec: AVIS CALL credits are an in-app usage meter, not a data plan. The code never claims otherwise — the low-credit and zero-credit strings match your spec exactly, and a user with no internet connection sees "No Internet Connection" and gets queued messages, never a fake "sent."
